.TH HD-SECBOOT-ENROLL 8 "October 2026" "hd-secboot" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-secboot-enroll \- enroll the hyprdebian Secure Boot keys in the firmware
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-secboot-enroll
.RB [ \-\-replace ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-secboot-enroll
enrolls the Secure Boot keys in
.I /etc/hyprdebian/secboot
in the firmware.
See
.BR hd-secboot (7)
for the keys.
.PP
The firmware must be in setup mode: without a platform key.
Most firmware has an entry in its setup menu, under Secure Boot, to delete
the platform key, to clear all keys, or to reset to setup mode.
.PP
Run it as root.
.SH OPERATION
.IP 1. 4
It checks that the signed updates
.IR db.auth ,
.I KEK.auth
and
.I PK.auth
exist, that the system was booted with UEFI, and that the firmware is in
setup mode.
Otherwise it stops before it changes anything.
.IP 2. 4
It writes db, then KEK, with
.BR efi-updatevar (1).
By default it adds the keys to the entries the firmware already has in
those variables, such as the certificates of Microsoft, which some
graphics cards and other devices need to load their firmware.
With
.BR \-\-replace ,
only the hyprdebian keys are left.
.IP 3. 4
It writes PK last, since setting the platform key ends setup mode.
There is only one platform key, so PK is always replaced.
.IP 4. 4
It checks that the firmware has left setup mode.
.PP
Before each write, it clears the immutable flag that the kernel puts on the
variables in
.IR /sys/firmware/efi/efivars .
When a write fails, the firmware may not let the system write its keys;
enroll the
.I .auth
or
.I .cer
files from its setup menu instead.
.PP
Secure Boot itself is turned on in the setup menu of the firmware, after
the EFI files that are to boot are signed with the db key.
Until then, the system boots as before.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Replace the entries in db and KEK instead of adding to them.
Only use this when every device in the system boots without the
certificates of Microsoft.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show the commands that would be run, and change nothing.
The checks of step 1 still run.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the keys were enrolled, 1 on an error, 2 on wrong arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/secboot
The keys.
.TP
.I /sys/firmware/efi/efivars
The EFI variables, among them SetupMode, PK, KEK and db.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what would be done, then enroll the keys:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-secboot-enroll \-\-dry\-run
hd-secboot-enroll
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-secboot (7),
.BR efi-updatevar (1),
.BR sbsign (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Erik Van Hamme <erik.vanhamme@gmail.com>
